<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hofuf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Al-Hofuf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hofuf is the main city of the al-Ahsa oasis in the Eastern Province of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, one of the largest palm oases in the world. It was the seat of the Ottoman province of Lahsa from the 16th century and of the Banu Khalid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1913, and its Ibrahim Palace and old market survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hofuf.jpg" alt="Hofuf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Dowley from Dubai, United Arab Emirates · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qasr_Sahoud_(4185899050).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1670 their chief Barrak ibn Ghurayr expelled the Ottoman garrison of the Lahsa Eyalet and made <b>Hofuf</b> the centre of an emirate controlling the coast from Kuwait to Qatar and the oases of al-Hasa and Qatif.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khalid-emirate">Banu Khalid Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its site has never been identified with certainty; Thaj, <b>Hofuf</b> and the coast near Uqair have all been proposed.</blockquote><a href="/state/gerrha">Gerrha</a></li><li><blockquote>The province of Lahsa was organised around 1560 with its seat at <b>Hofuf</b> and included the port of Qatif.</blockquote><a href="/state/lahsa-eyalet">Lahsa Eyalet</a></li></ul></section>
